WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
(soft gentle music)

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.370
- No country on Earth

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:05.703
has more military bases
abroad than the United States,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.400
with bases in places like
the Caribbean, the Azores,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.040
then countless ones all over Europe,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.040
stretching into the Arabian
Peninsula to Kenya and Africa.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.770
Diego Garcia in the Indian Ocean,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.770
Singapore in Southeast Asia, to Australia,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.323
and dozens of bases in Japan and Korea.

00:00:32.315 --> 00:00:34.890
In total, the US operates bases

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.490
in more than 70 countries
spanning the entire globe

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.763
and is able to project
power virtually everywhere.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.400
But there is one base

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.770
that is completely
different from all of them

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.530
because usually these bases
are in allied countries

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.510
with which the US has good relations

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.430
and military cooperation.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.080
But not this one.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.750
The US base in Cuba.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.075
It is called Guantanamo Bay,
and unlike the other bases,

00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:03.180
this one is located in a country

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.880
that the US is in conflict with.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.330
The US considers Cuba a
state sponsor of terrorism,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.017
and Cuba considers the US
presence in their country illegal.

00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:14.850
(light pulsing music)

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.210
The base is a place of extreme contrast.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.900
Some parts of it look so much
like a typical American city,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.606
and a pretty nice one, actually.

00:01:23.606 --> 00:01:26.070
There are schools, sports fields.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.040
There's a McDonald's where
you can "liberate your day"

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.810
by eating a McMuffin.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.660
And then there are these
suburban-style houses

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.810
where US military personnel can live.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.320
There's even a somewhat
makeshift nine-hole golf course

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.510
right on the base.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.360
The greens, little islands
surrounded by the dirt.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.337
- I'd have to say this is probably

00:01:47.337 --> 00:01:49.320
the strangest course I've played.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.940
It is very challenging.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.300
- But right next to this
seemingly typical American town,

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.070
there are large detention camps

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.310
housing some of the US's
highest-security prisoners,

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.505
camps that have caused political
controversy over decades.

00:02:03.505 --> 00:02:06.090
For this extensive documentary,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.591
I set myself the goal to
understand this unique place

00:02:09.591 --> 00:02:12.457
because Guantanamo Bay
plays an important role

00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:16.713
that goes far beyond the headlines
during the War on Terror.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.610
This is America's most
controversial base, explained.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.228
So here's the deal.

00:02:26.228 --> 00:02:29.400
I'm building up to
something with this video.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.630
Throughout this story,

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.700
I will be trying to view Guantanamo Bay

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.140
as a sort of metaphor

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.390
because I believe that this military base

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.958
can represent the complicated relationship

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:42.001
between the United States
and the rest of the world.

00:02:42.001 --> 00:02:46.113
And I've chosen four
themes to guide this idea.

00:02:46.113 --> 00:02:48.288
First, there is independence,

00:02:48.288 --> 00:02:52.375
a word that means so much in
how the US defines itself,

00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:55.993
but also how it views its
ties with other nations,

00:02:55.993 --> 00:02:58.800
and independence is the perfect keyword

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.644
to lead us to our first question.

00:03:00.644 --> 00:03:03.810
How did the US acquire this piece of land

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.298
in the first place, and why
is there a US base in Cuba?

00:03:08.298 --> 00:03:09.990
(light thoughtful music)

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.783
The answer, not surprisingly, is by force,

00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:16.983
but not by war with Cuba,
but instead with Spain.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.680
Christopher Columbus had
claimed Cuba for Spain in 1492,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.560
and for centuries it was
part of the Spanish Empire,

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.390
which at its largest extent

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.093
stretched all across the Americas.

00:03:30.093 --> 00:03:31.797
But in the late 1800s,

00:03:31.797 --> 00:03:34.969
the Spanish hold had
significantly decreased,

00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:37.020
and Cuba and Puerto Rico

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.650
remained the last Spanish
holds in the region.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.604
Cubans began to fight for independence,

00:03:42.604 --> 00:03:46.080
and for years, the United
States watched on the sidelines

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.982
until they couldn't anymore.

00:03:47.982 --> 00:03:49.380
(light pulsing music)

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.960
Disturbing reports began making their way

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.763
into the US news cycle.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.880
Cuban rebels fighting for independence

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.811
were getting support from local
farmers who provided housing

00:03:59.811 --> 00:04:02.791
and food to the resistance fighters.

00:04:02.791 --> 00:04:04.800
And to put a stop to this,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.950
Spain decides to move anyone
who lives in the countryside

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.550
into literal concentration camps.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.248
Anyone who refuses to relocate
to these camps is killed,

00:04:15.248 --> 00:04:17.100
and within these camps,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.411
diseases and starvation quickly spread.

00:04:20.411 --> 00:04:23.850
Hundreds of thousands of
Cubans die in these camps,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.160
and the American public
becomes sympathetic

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.720
as they hear more horror stories

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.640
about what their neighbors across the Gulf

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.140
are being subjected to,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.500
and they begin to ask their
government to step in.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.240
The pressure from the American public

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.550
and the desire to push the last remnants

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.100
of European powers out of the Americas

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.433
finally leads to the United
States joining the fight.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.080
Just a little over 120 years earlier,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.260
the US had fought its
own war for independence,

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.233
but now it is helping Cuba do the same.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.980
The war doesn't last long after this.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.940
Spain agrees to give up its
last remaining colonies,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.553
and suddenly, Cuba is freed.

00:05:05.553 --> 00:05:07.140
(gentle contemplative music)

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.020
Except, actually, it isn't.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.375
The treaty between the
United States and Spain

00:05:12.375 --> 00:05:14.694
guarantees the independence of Cuba,

00:05:14.694 --> 00:05:18.540
but it allows the US to occupy the island.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.731
In turn, the US makes clear to Cuba

00:05:20.731 --> 00:05:23.220
that it will only withdraw its troops

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.270
if Cuba agrees to its own
treaty with the United States,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.970
a treaty that includes
leasing land to the US

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.260
for a naval station,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.830
the very one that would
become Guantanamo Bay.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.410
Cuba, in no position to negotiate,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:41.580
has little choice but to
agree, and the lease is signed.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.920
A lease that has no expiration date

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.453
and can only be terminated
if both countries agree.

00:05:47.453 --> 00:05:49.140
(gentle thoughtful music)

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.900
With the Guantanamo Bay
Naval Base established,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.520
the United States begins sending

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.534
annual payments for the lease to Cuba

00:05:55.534 --> 00:06:00.030
in the amount of $2,000
paid in gold coins,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.631
and the US establishes a
permanent camp on the island.

00:06:03.631 --> 00:06:06.540
They build housing for military personnel,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.690
and they even built a baseball field.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.970
The US has fought for Cuban freedom,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:12.990
but it made sure

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.720
that it can shape what
that freedom looks like,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.190
and it will not take long

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.683
until the relationship
changes drastically.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.640
(tense uneasy music)

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.800
With Cuba now left to its own devices,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.600
a series of various presidents

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.280
and dictators rule the country,

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.760
leading up to US-backed
dictator Fulgencio Batista.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.880
Here he is with US President Eisenhower.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.820
For the US, the situation
couldn't be much better.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.490
Guantanamo Bay becomes an important port

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.160
for the United States
Navy and the Caribbean.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.180
In 1931, a grass runway
is set up by the bay.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.120
A decade later, that runway is paved,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.280
and another runway is built
on the other side of the bay,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.280
which is eventually extended

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.310
so it can accommodate newer jet aircraft.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.290
- [News Reporter] On the
southeast coast of Cuba,

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:04.020
Guantanamo's commodious
and well-sheltered harbor

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.660
can accommodate the entire US fleet.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.210
- The base's presence
is tolerated by Cuba;

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:11.340
after all, it even helps their economy,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.750
with thousands of workers

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.013
crossing the border to the base every day.

00:07:15.013 --> 00:07:17.081
- [News Reporter] 1,200
United States Marines

00:07:17.081 --> 00:07:18.060
are sailing aboard the
Army transport Barnett

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.440
for winter training in Guantanamo, Cuba.

00:07:22.148 --> 00:07:26.148
US Devil Dogs off to
safeguard our new outposts.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.879
- However, after just a few
decades of peaceful coexistence,

00:07:30.879 --> 00:07:32.700
everything changes.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.250
In 1959, Fidel Castro

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.430
successfully overthrows
Batista's dictatorship,

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:40.976
and he installs a new communist regime,

00:07:40.976 --> 00:07:43.860
one that quickly becomes very friendly

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.330
with the Soviet Union,

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.000
the arch-nemesis to the United States.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.900
And with that, the Cold War
between these two superpowers

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.700
has reached Guantanamo Bay.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.380
- [News Reporter] Castro
marks the second anniversary

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.870
of his revolution with the
biggest military parade

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.430
ever staged in Cuba.

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:02.370
President Eisenhower broke
off diplomatic relations

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.070
with the message read by
Press Secretary Haggerty.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:08.430
- Meanwhile, our sympathy
goes out to the people of Cuba

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.150
now suffering under
the yoke of a dictator.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.562
- After decades of
toleration for the base,

00:08:14.562 --> 00:08:18.720
Cuba's new leader, Castro,
has a very clear demand.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.510
He wants the land back for Cuba.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.720
Since, according to the treaty,
both countries need to agree

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.907
and war against the powerful
United States is not an option,

00:08:27.907 --> 00:08:30.238
there is little Castro can do,

00:08:30.238 --> 00:08:34.440
except perhaps turn to the Soviet Union.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.224
Strengthening this new
alliance might offer protection

00:08:37.224 --> 00:08:41.313
and, just maybe, enough leverage
to push the Americans out.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:43.860
For the typical American,

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.620
the tensions between the United
States and the Soviet Union

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.150
had so far only been felt
far away from home turf,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.250
proxy wars in Asia

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.420
with American and Soviet forces
supporting opposite sides,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.660
and heavy militarization in Europe.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:04.710
But Cuba's alliance with the
Soviet Union changes this.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:09.429
Suddenly, the Cold War was just
90 miles away from Florida.

00:09:09.429 --> 00:09:12.051
As the iron curtain is
placed down in Europe

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:16.410
in the form of walls and fences,
another iron curtain rises

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.500
just outside the border of Guantanamo Bay.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.040
Except this one is made of cactus.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.690
Cuban troops are deployed

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:28.344
to plant a large cactus strip
around the American base.

00:09:28.344 --> 00:09:30.030
(lively orchestral music)

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:31.080
- [News Reporter] The Cubans have planted

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:32.825
a cactus curtain strip around the base.

00:09:32.825 --> 00:09:35.831
75 feet wide, it's a vigorous plant,

00:09:35.831 --> 00:09:38.623
as effective as a wire fence.

00:09:38.623 --> 00:09:40.410
Looking at the base today,

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.780
you can see the outline right here,

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.510
and then you see a second
perimeter around it

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.430
on the Cuban side.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.280
The term "cactus curtain"
is used to this day

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.680
to describe the border between the base

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.153
and the rest of Cuba.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.570
But the cactus border
was just the beginning,

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.210
because both the Cuban and
the American militaries

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:04.533
planned an even deadlier
deterrent: landmines.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.723
Around 55,000 mines are
placed in this strip,

00:10:10.677 --> 00:10:14.193
making it one of the largest
minefields of its time.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.860
With tension so high,

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.230
it feels like it's only a matter of time

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:22.200
before something drastic happens.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.727
And so it does.

00:10:23.727 --> 00:10:25.560
(tense uneasy music)

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.240
In response to the United States

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.940
placing nuclear missiles
in Italy and Turkey,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.450
where they are clearly within
the reach of the Soviet Union,

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.270
the Soviet Union now does the same,

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:40.020
and it stations its own
nuclear missiles in Cuba.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:43.350
The US publishes a series of photos

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.107
showing these missile bases in Cuba,

00:10:46.107 --> 00:10:48.600
and the situation escalates.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.109
- The presence of these large, long-range,

00:10:51.109 --> 00:10:55.230
and clearly offensive weapons
of sudden mass destruction

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.810
constitutes an explicit
threat to the peace

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.260
and security of all the Americas.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.570
To halt this offensive buildup,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.613
a strict quarantine on all
offensive military equipment

00:11:06.613 --> 00:11:09.690
under shipment to Cuba is being initiated.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.250
- [News Reporter] The US
threw up a steel fence,

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.313
prepared to stop any vessel
carrying materials of war.

00:11:15.535 --> 00:11:17.637
- [Host] Both the US and the Soviet Union

00:11:17.637 --> 00:11:20.532
are now within striking
distance of each other,

00:11:20.532 --> 00:11:25.530
and nuclear war seems
closer than ever before.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.993
Immediately, alarms ring out

00:11:26.993 --> 00:11:29.400
through the base of Guantanamo Bay,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.280
ordering all civilians to evacuate.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.080
- [News Reporter] Over
3,000 Navy and Marine

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.590
wives and children are
evacuated from Guantanamo.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:39.870
- With little time, pets are left behind,

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:43.830
and each person carries only
one suitcase of belongings,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.870
not knowing if they will ever be able

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:48.201
to return to their homes again.

00:11:48.201 --> 00:11:53.201
The evacuees join the world
as everyone watches and waits,

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:57.780
until at last everyone can
finally breathe easy once again.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.310
- [News Reporter] Soviet
Chairman Khrushchev

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.800
decided to dismantle the bases in Cuba

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.170
and to remove the offensive weapons.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.240
The missiles that had threatened the US

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.574
went back where they came from.

00:12:08.574 --> 00:12:12.240
- The Soviet Union agreed to
take its missiles out of Cuba

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.910
in exchange for the United States to agree

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:19.910
not to attack or invade Cuba,
as well as a secret deal

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.231
to also remove its missiles from Turkey.

00:12:23.231 --> 00:12:26.070
(bright thoughtful music)

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:28.020
Though the immediate crisis is over

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.256
and civilians are able
to return to their homes,

00:12:30.256 --> 00:12:32.827
not everything is back to normal.

00:12:32.827 --> 00:12:34.935
Castro still wants the base gone,

00:12:34.935 --> 00:12:37.050
and he starts to reject the checks

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.210
that the United States
has been sending regularly

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.856
to pay for the lease.

00:12:40.856 --> 00:12:42.865
He also shuts down a pipeline

00:12:42.865 --> 00:12:45.870
that supplies the base with fresh water.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:49.080
Castro offers to supply
one hour of water per day

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:53.520
for humanitarian needs, but
not anymore beyond that.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.430
This offer is rejected by the Americans.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.010
Instead, tanker ships filled with water

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.810
are sent to Guantanamo

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.709
while workers construct
a desalination plant.

00:13:03.709 --> 00:13:06.090
To this day, the base has to operate

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.310
autonomous from the rest of Cuba.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.800
Ocean water is turned into drinking water

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.891
by two desalination plants.

00:13:12.891 --> 00:13:15.840
Electricity comes from
a diesel generator plant

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.132
as well as from wind turbines
that were installed in 2002,

00:13:19.132 --> 00:13:22.190
and supplies are shipped
in from the United States.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:23.970
(lively string music)

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.990
To this day, Cuba has rejected
every US lease payment

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.603
since the Castro family's
leadership began,

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.530
and that choice to stay
even when unwelcome

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:37.957
is not unique to Guantanamo
but can be seen as a constant

00:13:37.957 --> 00:13:41.100
in how the US tries to
build global presence,

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.960
as no other country on Earth

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.404
maintains such a vast military
footprint around the globe.

00:13:47.404 --> 00:13:51.390
And the challenges the United
States faces at Guantanamo Bay

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:55.383
are often the very same it
struggles with on a larger scale.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:59.970
This same pattern also
shows up in the next chapter

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.055
of the base: immigration.

00:14:02.055 --> 00:14:04.230
(gentle contemplative music)

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:08.340
In 1991, just a stone's throw
away from Guantanamo Bay,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:10.323
unrest in Haiti ramps up.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:12.570
The Haitian president

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.120
was overthrown by a military dictatorship,

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.430
and now thousands of Haitians are fleeing

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.760
using makeshift rafts, many
of whom are intercepted

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.270
by the US Coast Guard and
sent to Guantanamo Bay,

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:27.720
as the base soon becomes
the symbol of the crisis.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.370
- [News Reporter] The
US Coast Guard planes

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.820
and ships sighting several
hundred bodies so far.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:37.173
Number of survivors isn't
known, but it isn't large.

00:14:38.039 --> 00:14:40.710
- The US classifies the fleeing Haitians

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:44.130
as economic migrants, requiring
them to undergo screening

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:47.100
to determine if they qualify for asylum.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.290
And in order to carry out this process

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.570
without bringing them onto US soil,

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:53.640
Guantanamo Bay becomes the base

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:56.287
where their cases are reviewed.

00:14:56.287 --> 00:14:59.906
The camp grows quickly,
with thousands of Haitians

00:14:59.906 --> 00:15:02.883
packed into a space
never meant to hold them.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:07.620
The old airfield is turned
into a sprawling tent city,

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.189
one that soon grew enormously,

00:15:10.189 --> 00:15:13.173
as visible by these satellite pictures.

00:15:14.127 --> 00:15:17.250
And even this space was not enough.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.280
The golf course nearby
was repurposed as well

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.460
to become a temporary tent city.

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:26.250
The base soon reaches maximum capacity,

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.580
and the decision is made that
anyone else encountered at sea

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:31.501
will skip processing altogether

00:15:31.501 --> 00:15:34.623
and is forcibly sent back to Haiti.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:38.329
- [News Reporter] They fled
Haiti in hope of a better life.

00:15:38.329 --> 00:15:41.253
They're returning now
in fear of their lives.

00:15:42.734 --> 00:15:45.634
- As for those that did
make it to Guantanamo,

00:15:45.634 --> 00:15:48.300
they don't fare much better.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:51.330
Inside the perimeter, life is hard.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:55.500
Trash piles up, and food is
often unevenly distributed

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:59.553
as overcrowding strains the
water and electricity supply.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:04.975
Even still, throughout this
period between 1991 and 1995,

00:16:04.975 --> 00:16:07.259
many at Guantanamo did their best

00:16:07.259 --> 00:16:10.263
to improve the living
conditions of the refugees.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.530
- We've had complaints of the food,

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:14.900
and it did look pretty dismal to us.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:16.497
- It is dismal.

00:16:16.497 --> 00:16:18.510
I will make no apologies for the fact

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.190
that the food is not good; it isn't good.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.040
As we get more food in, we will
start to vary the menu more,

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:25.830
but right now, the
complaints are legitimate.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:27.750
- But with a situation thrust upon them

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:30.780
with little logistical support
from the US government,

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.780
they simply have to make
do with limited funding

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:34.863
and resources.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.578
The residents of each small camp

00:16:37.578 --> 00:16:39.830
got to elect their own camp leaders,

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:42.870
who in turn meet regularly
with the generals

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.138
to discuss possible improvements.

00:16:45.138 --> 00:16:48.630
Following these meetings,
residents are given more autonomy,

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:52.470
for example, having a say in
meal planning and preparation.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.173
And inside the camps, the
military police patrol unarmed,

00:16:56.173 --> 00:16:59.190
carrying only a radio,

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.629
a sign both of the residents' peacefulness

00:17:01.629 --> 00:17:05.310
and of an effort to appear
less like prison guards

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:07.493
and more like peacekeepers.

00:17:07.493 --> 00:17:08.850
But the frustration

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:11.520
that the individual
service members can't ease

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.718
comes from the fact that
the camp's residents

00:17:13.718 --> 00:17:17.274
receive no answers about
what will happen next.

00:17:17.274 --> 00:17:21.783
And even the military itself
must wait on the White House.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:27.142
Life in these refugee
camps was extremely hard.

00:17:27.142 --> 00:17:30.960
Rows of cots stretch
out on concrete slabs,

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:34.260
sometimes laying directly in the dirt,

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.060
and if someone causes trouble,

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.733
they are taken to a different location.

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:43.860
Roughly 4 1/2 miles east of the airfield,

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.320
a makeshift jail is built.

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:48.990
Its name is Camp X-Ray.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:50.760
You can see the fencing around it

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:54.180
as well as multiple watchtowers
encircling the camp.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.610
From this view, it might
appear that these roofs

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:59.760
belong to buildings housing the prisoners,

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.370
but that is not what they are.

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:04.121
This footage from 2021

00:18:04.121 --> 00:18:09.000
shows the by-now abandoned
Camp X-Ray from the ground.

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:10.800
As you can see when we zoom in,

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:14.880
the roofs mostly cover cages
where prisoners are kept,

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.290
there are no walls,

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:18.780
and the prisoners are
exposed to the elements

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:20.734
with no privacy.

00:18:20.734 --> 00:18:22.920
(light thoughtful music)

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:24.960
But these people at Camp X-Ray

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.500
were not the only ones
separated from the main camp;

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:31.363
going south, there is yet
another camp being built:

00:18:31.363 --> 00:18:33.270
Camp Bulkeley.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.220
The early '90s was a time during which

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:38.301
a new fear began spreading like wildfire.

00:18:38.301 --> 00:18:41.490
Fear of HIV.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.020
The spread of the epidemic

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:46.110
led the American Center for
Disease Control and Prevention

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:47.643
to falsely label Haitians

00:18:47.643 --> 00:18:52.347
as a group with increased
risk for contracting HIV.

00:18:52.347 --> 00:18:53.910
And because of this label,

00:18:53.910 --> 00:18:56.130
Haitians are screened much more intensely

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:58.680
than any other nationality for the virus,

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:02.520
with every Haitian national
required to undergo testing.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.640
And if someone does test
positive in Guantanamo Bay,

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.040
they are removed from
the general population

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.380
and sent to Camp Bulkeley,

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.393
and the refugees are given bracelets

00:19:12.393 --> 00:19:14.430
that they are required to wear,

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:17.823
identifying them as carriers of HIV.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:23.205
Out of over 20,000 Haitians,
a total of 276 people

00:19:23.205 --> 00:19:26.220
are ultimately quarantined
in Camp Bulkeley,

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.890
with little access to proper healthcare,

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.890
and with their fate also unknown.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.010
These people are all individuals
who have passed a screening

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.290
to be granted asylum to the United States.

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:41.580
But a 1987 law prohibits anyone with HIV

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.684
from entering the country.

00:19:43.684 --> 00:19:47.040
This puts them into legal limbo.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.260
And when asked by an AP reporter

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:51.240
why they cannot enter the US,

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.430
a spokesperson for the US Immigration

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.777
and Naturalization Service replied,

00:19:55.777 --> 00:19:58.077
"They're going to die
anyway, aren't they?"

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:03.712
Weeks turn into months,
which turns into years,

00:20:03.712 --> 00:20:06.030
and after two years have passed,

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.310
the Haitians imprisoned in Camp Bulkeley

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:10.590
still don't know their fate,

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:15.030
until finally a US federal
court rules that Camp Bulkeley

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.970
is essentially an HIV prison camp,

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:21.720
and it orders those living
there to be evacuated.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.300
Within half a year, Camp
Bulkeley's residents

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:26.275
are granted entry to the United States,

00:20:26.275 --> 00:20:28.811
and the camp officially closes.

00:20:28.811 --> 00:20:32.340
As for the rest of the Haitians
waiting in Guantanamo Bay,

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:35.292
they are soon joined by a
different group of refugees,

00:20:35.292 --> 00:20:37.101
as yet another crisis

00:20:37.101 --> 00:20:40.560
led to a second influx
of people on the base.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.441
This time from Cuba itself.

00:20:43.441 --> 00:20:45.450
After the fall of the Soviet Union,

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.690
Cuba slipped into an economic
crisis, sparking protests

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.880
with people demanding
better living conditions

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.680
and political change.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:57.510
In 1994, this led to a massive
surge in attempts by Cubans

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:00.473
to reach Florida on makeshift rafts.

00:21:00.473 --> 00:21:03.330
Those who managed to make
it to the United States

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:05.340
were offered legal asylum,

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:09.000
and at its peak, as many as 33,000 people

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:10.829
set out on this journey.

00:21:10.829 --> 00:21:13.230
In response, the Clinton administration

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:15.134
began taking action to stop it

00:21:15.134 --> 00:21:18.660
and decides that any
rafters intercepted at sea

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:22.545
will also be taken to Guantanamo,
adding even more stress

00:21:22.545 --> 00:21:26.100
to the already overcrowded refugee camp.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.380
- [News Reporter] Guantanamo
Bay is filling fast.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:31.680
Yesterday alone, a record 2,300 rafters

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:35.060
were plucked from the Straits
of Florida and brought here.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:37.440
- [Host] Eventually, the
Clinton administration

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:40.650
even goes so far to change policy.

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:44.130
While it used to be that any
Cuban rescued on the waters

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:47.614
by the US Coast Guard would
get to enter the United States,

00:21:47.614 --> 00:21:48.930
under the new policy,

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.570
only those who actually make it to Florida

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.308
with their self-made rafts get to stay;

00:21:54.308 --> 00:21:57.810
everyone else will be sent back to Cuba.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:59.340
- [News Reporter] Picking
up those Cuban rafters,

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:01.170
still putting out to sea for America,

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.450
and then doing what would've
been unthinkable before this,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:05.580
pulling into port in still communist Cuba

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.550
and handing those refugees
back to Castro's police,

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:10.863
something America's
never ever done before.

00:22:11.734 --> 00:22:14.068
(gentle contemplative music)

00:22:14.068 --> 00:22:17.220
- [Host] For the Cubans
already at Guantanamo, though,

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.950
a lengthy legal and
political battle took place

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:21.064
to decide their fate

00:22:21.064 --> 00:22:24.153
as well as the fate of
the Haitian refugees.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:28.620
At one point during this time,
a couple of Cuban detainees

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:30.630
managed to escape from their camp,

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:33.270
and they occupy a church on the base

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:36.150
located right next to the McDonald's.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.730
These Cubans were eventually brought back,

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.790
and the area was cordoned
off by the military.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:46.790
But the situation led to this
fascinating photo being taken.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:51.510
The McDonald's at Guantanamo
Bay, surrounded by barbed wire,

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:53.992
a photo that became a powerful symbol

00:22:53.992 --> 00:22:56.523
of the base's contradictions.

00:22:58.004 --> 00:23:02.820
In 1995, after years of
turmoil and waiting in limbo,

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:06.270
the refugees can finally leave Guantanamo,

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:08.733
and all of the camps are closed.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.570
By the mid-'90s, Guantanamo
had become a symbol

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.510
of how the United States
handles immigration,

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.289
a reflection of a system that even today

00:23:18.289 --> 00:23:21.000
remains one of the country's most divisive

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:22.710
and unresolved issues.

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:25.020
- Given the nature of
this country politically,

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:27.180
do you have any hope that
this immigration question

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:30.570
is gonna get resolved within
this decade, Senator Simpson?

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:31.473
- I really do.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:35.400
- [Host] And the use of the
base as a place to hold migrants

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:38.550
has only recently
returned to the headlines

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:41.610
as President Trump announced
plans to once again

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:44.790
use Guantanamo Bay as a migrant facility.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:49.470
- We have 30,000 beds in Guantanamo

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:51.506
to detain the worst
criminal illegal aliens

00:23:51.506 --> 00:23:53.490
threatening the American people.

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:55.860
And tough.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.739
It's a tough place to get out of.

00:23:57.739 --> 00:24:01.350
- After the announcement,
some tent camps were set up,

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:04.348
and several hundred migrants,
mostly from Venezuela,

00:24:04.348 --> 00:24:07.321
were held at the base
for a period of time,

00:24:07.321 --> 00:24:09.027
but it was only temporary.

00:24:09.027 --> 00:24:11.040
And whether these announced plans

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.223
will actually be carried
out remains to be seen.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:17.932
And while Guantanamo made
headlines in the 1990s

00:24:17.932 --> 00:24:19.530
as a refugee camp,

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:22.050
in the early 2000s, the
camp becomes the spotlight

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:27.050
for a different challenge that
will define an entire decade.

00:24:27.062 --> 00:24:29.100
(tense thoughtful music)

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.280
- The United States
military has begun strikes

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.213
against Al-Qaeda terrorist training camps

00:24:34.213 --> 00:24:35.643
and military installations

00:24:35.643 --> 00:24:38.070
of the Taliban regime in Afghanistan.

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:40.230
- [News Reporter] We know
of targets in Kandahar

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:43.563
and Kabul for certain,
some 50 cruise missiles.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:47.247
- [Host] After the attacks of 2001,

00:24:47.247 --> 00:24:50.880
the United States began an
invasion of Afghanistan,

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.950
and on the ground, the American military

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:57.630
begins taking prisoners,
except now there is a problem.

00:24:57.630 --> 00:24:59.970
Many of the prisoners captured by the US

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:02.130
were suspected to be terrorists,

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.050
but the actual amount of evidence

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:08.610
to legally justify detaining
these people was often small.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:11.850
If they are sent back to the
United States for a trial,

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:15.267
it could be very likely that
they are found not guilty.

00:25:15.267 --> 00:25:18.180
And so once again, the American government

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:20.530
turns its eye towards
its previous location

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:24.423
to deal with tricky
situations: Guantanamo Bay.

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:27.090
If a prisoner is sent here,

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:30.090
they can still be imprisoned
by the American military,

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.340
but without any of the protections

00:25:32.340 --> 00:25:35.430
that being in the United States
would otherwise give them,

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:37.897
such as the right to a fair trial.

00:25:37.897 --> 00:25:41.370
And so, almost immediately,
suspected terrorists

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:45.120
are shipped across the world to this base.

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:47.650
- Terrorists who once occupied Afghanistan

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:51.673
now occupy cells at Guantanamo Bay.

00:25:51.673 --> 00:25:56.673
(audience applauding)
(gentle contemplative music)

00:25:56.850 --> 00:25:59.130
- [Host] Guantanamo Bay
was not well-equipped

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:01.470
to become a terrorist prisoner camp,

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.150
and so the decision is made

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:05.280
to use the temporary infrastructure

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.908
of an already existing camp, Camp X-Ray,

00:26:08.908 --> 00:26:12.480
the same location where Haitian
and Cuban refugees were sent

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.840
if they caused issues
during the refugee crisis.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.730
- The heat here is intense,

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:20.816
and the guards say that it's
baking inside the prison cells

00:26:20.816 --> 00:26:23.643
with their concrete
floors and wire caging.

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:27.360
- These photographs
showing the first prisoners

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:30.353
being taken to the camp
were taken by the military,

00:26:30.353 --> 00:26:34.020
but they had not been published for years.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.520
Only through a New York Times

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.500
Freedom of Information Act request,

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:39.993
they became public in 2022.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:44.488
The prisoners are shackled,
their hands and ears covered.

00:26:44.488 --> 00:26:48.060
They are essentially
deprived of their senses.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:50.940
One soldier plants an American flag

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:52.920
in a prisoner's shackled hands.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.500
And other photos show
the medical facilities

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.810
where prisoners are checked.

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:00.296
This photo shows a prisoner facing away

00:27:00.296 --> 00:27:02.343
as a guard brings food.

00:27:03.403 --> 00:27:05.340
(light pulsing music)

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:06.629
After about four months,

00:27:06.629 --> 00:27:10.140
the detainees are moved to
a new permanent location

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.030
that is set up at the same place

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.010
where the HIV camp used to be

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:16.860
during the refugee crisis in the '90s.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:19.242
This camp is now called Camp Delta.

00:27:19.242 --> 00:27:22.719
Over the coming years, this
camp is continuously expanded

00:27:22.719 --> 00:27:26.400
and is further broken down
into several sub-camps,

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:29.850
ranging from low- to
high-security prisons.

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.760
A tiered system where the
more compliant prisoner

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:36.270
is moved to a lower-security
camp that has more freedoms,

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:39.663
while the difficult detainees
are kept at higher security.

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:42.270
In the higher-security camps,

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:45.930
detainees are held in
virtual solitary confinement.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:49.320
They're allowed only two hours
of recreation time each day

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:51.150
in individual pens

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:54.832
with no interaction allowed
with the other prisoners.

00:27:54.832 --> 00:27:55.932
(light thoughtful music)

00:27:55.932 --> 00:27:59.850
A little further east, also
located directly by the ocean,

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:03.001
Camp Iguana is set up
to house three prisoners

00:28:03.001 --> 00:28:06.030
that were below the age of 16.

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.680
Though later it was confirmed

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:10.500
that there were other underage
prisoners at Guantanamo

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:12.933
that were not held at Camp Iguana.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:17.367
This photo shows the
recreation yard at Camp Iguana

00:28:17.367 --> 00:28:19.083
facing the sea.

00:28:19.947 --> 00:28:22.860
There's something fascinating
about the contrast

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:25.680
between the natural beauty of this place

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.293
and the harshness of this prison camp.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:31.650
With all the different camps,

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:33.180
there isn't a definitive system

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:37.032
that dictates when prisoners
should be moved between them.

00:28:37.032 --> 00:28:40.482
There are no trials and no review boards.

00:28:40.482 --> 00:28:42.420
(bright soft music)

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:43.620
The American government

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:45.870
claims that the prisoners at Guantanamo

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:48.500
are not protected by the US Constitution,

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:51.570
as they are not on US territory.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:53.490
But there is still international law

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:56.580
that dictates how a prisoner
of war ought to be treated.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:59.490
And one of the things prohibited
by the Geneva Conventions

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:03.393
is indefinite imprisonment
without charge or a trial.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:07.080
And so President Bush begins adopting

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:10.830
a simple use of wordplay
to skirt these restrictions

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:14.238
by labeling the detainees
as enemy combatants

00:29:14.238 --> 00:29:17.490
rather than prisoners of war.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:19.740
Several countries continually call

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:23.300
for the closure of Camp Delta
over human rights abuses,

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:26.846
including the use of torture on detainees.

00:29:26.846 --> 00:29:30.660
Eventually, the Bush
administration starts to backtrack.

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.240
The government wants to close Camp Delta,

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:36.600
but they can't as long as
there are still prisoners.

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:40.320
Hundreds of detainees are
released or transferred,

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:42.750
but many still remain.

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:45.360
Unless the US is willing to simply release

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:47.910
the remaining prisoners,
which they are not,

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:49.622
there has to be a trial,

00:29:49.622 --> 00:29:52.980
but you can't have a
trial without a courtroom,

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:55.290
of which there is none on the base.

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:58.799
And so the plan is made
to build Camp Justice.

00:29:58.799 --> 00:30:01.350
Initially, Camp Justice was intended to be

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:02.820
a permanent courthouse

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.433
that would've cost around $100 million US.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:10.500
However, Congress is not
willing to allow such a budget.

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:13.140
Politically, on the list of
things to spend money on,

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:17.820
a courthouse in Guantanamo is
about as unpopular as it gets.

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:19.800
Therefore, the budget is capped

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:23.373
at just 1/10 of the initial
proposal, $10 million.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:28.560
This isn't near enough to
construct a brand-new courthouse

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:32.910
on a military base detached
from the rest of the country.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.174
And so the plan is reworked,

00:30:35.174 --> 00:30:37.200
and instead of a solid building,

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:41.643
portable tents are set up yet
again on the old airfield.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:45.270
It looks not too dissimilar

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:48.573
to the tents that housed
refugees just a decade earlier.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:53.125
Within those tents, the
detainees are not given a trial

00:30:53.125 --> 00:30:56.280
like they would be entitled to in the US.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:58.724
Instead, they are military tribunals.

00:30:58.724 --> 00:31:00.598
Prisoners often don't have access

00:31:00.598 --> 00:31:04.110
to the evidence used against
them to offer a rebuttal.

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.540
Decisions don't need to be unanimous,

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:09.737
and many parts of the tribunal
are closed off to the public,

00:31:09.737 --> 00:31:13.686
sometimes even to the
defendant's own attorneys.

00:31:13.686 --> 00:31:18.296
But over the years, this tent
city has seen very little use.

00:31:18.296 --> 00:31:22.110
As of 2025, only eight
men have been convicted

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:24.330
since Camp Justice has been established,

00:31:24.330 --> 00:31:27.960
and half of those convictions
have since been overturned.

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:29.460
- I have opposed the president

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:33.453
on spending on climate change,
on torture of prisoners,

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:36.617
on Guantanamo Bay.

00:31:36.617 --> 00:31:40.121
- I said repeatedly that I
intend to close Guantanamo,

00:31:40.121 --> 00:31:41.880
and I will follow through on that.

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.197
- Barack Obama and John McCain said,

00:31:44.197 --> 00:31:46.170
"We need to close Guantanamo,"

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:49.081
but we all forgot to ask
the next question: How?

00:31:49.081 --> 00:31:50.760
(light thoughtful music)

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:53.820
- Most of the hundreds of
prisoners held in Camp Delta

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:56.490
have instead been transferred
to other countries,

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:59.160
or they have been released with no charges

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:01.830
after being held for years on end.

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:03.900
And as of the making of this video,

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:06.651
only 15 men are remaining.

00:32:06.651 --> 00:32:08.490
The cost per prisoner

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:11.760
at the highest-security
federal prisons in the US

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:15.030
is $78,000 per year.

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:17.940
Based on official numbers from 2015,

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:19.530
we know the cost of running

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:22.080
just the prison facility
at Guantanamo Bay,

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:24.630
and based on the current
population of prisoners,

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:26.940
their cost per prisoner at Guantanamo

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:30.693
comes out to a staggering $29 million US.

00:32:32.023 --> 00:32:35.523
(tense thoughtful music)

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:39.660
The term "Guantanamo Bay"

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:42.240
is collectively linked
to the War on Terror,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:46.170
as the camp continues to be
controversial to this day.

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:48.840
But really, this is just
one of many chapters

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:53.840
in the base's history that
all follow one common theme,

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.670
as the base is a reflection of
America's uneasy relationship

00:32:57.670 --> 00:32:59.673
with the rest of the world.

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:03.003
By wanting to help,

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:07.720
but also wanting to control,

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.900
(explosion booms)

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:13.020
By surrounding others,

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:15.453
but yet feeling surrounded.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:22.040
By offering opportunity
while building fences.

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:27.630
And by defending ideals

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:31.740
while sometimes suspending
them when convenient.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.470
And as a place full of contradictions,

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:36.840
the history of this base holds a mirror

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:40.083
to over a century of
foreign policy decisions.

00:33:41.405 --> 00:33:43.983
This is Guantanamo Bay.

00:33:44.957 --> 00:33:46.548
(bright chiming music)

00:33:46.548 --> 00:33:50.790
The War on Terror didn't just
shape places like Guantanamo;

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:52.756
it shaped an entire generation.

00:33:52.756 --> 00:33:55.380
It's one of those topics that's so huge,

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:57.090
you can only really understand it

00:33:57.090 --> 00:33:58.500
by looking at all the smaller,

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:00.483
stranger stories that came out of it.

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:05.910
One of my favorite shows
recently is "Mad Kings."

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:08.910
It covers some of the world's
most unhinged dictators

00:34:08.910 --> 00:34:10.322
and corrupt leaders.

00:34:10.322 --> 00:34:12.300
For example, one episode tells

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.153
the absolutely crazy life story of Uday,

00:34:15.153 --> 00:34:17.490
one of Saddam Hussein's sons,

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:20.229
who committed thousands
upon thousands of crimes

00:34:20.229 --> 00:34:22.800
during his father's reign in power,

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:25.744
a documentary that would
immediately get demonetized

00:34:25.744 --> 00:34:28.143
or age-restricted on YouTube,

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:32.097
which is why it was made
exclusively for Nebula.

00:34:32.097 --> 00:34:32.930
(computer mouse clicks)

00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:34.170
(bright thoughtful music)

00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:36.630
Nebula is a streaming
service that I helped build

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:38.880
together with a bunch
of like-minded creators,

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:41.280
because we wanted to make bigger, weirder,

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:45.518
and more meaningful stuff than
what usually fits on YouTube.

00:34:45.518 --> 00:34:48.349
It is where I uploaded my 3D short film

00:34:48.349 --> 00:34:51.184
about the refugee crisis

00:34:51.184 --> 00:34:54.720
and my exclusive series "Under Exposure,"

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:56.490
which covers darker subject matter,

00:34:56.490 --> 00:34:59.433
such as one episode
about the Bin Laden raid.

00:35:00.342 --> 00:35:03.882
Everything on Nebula is made
by independent creators,

00:35:03.882 --> 00:35:06.277
which honestly makes it way more creative

00:35:06.277 --> 00:35:09.150
than any other streaming
service I know of,

00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:11.850
with documentaries, game shows,

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:15.647
and really incredible film projects.

00:35:15.647 --> 00:35:19.107
I'm really excited about what
we're building over on Nebula.

00:35:19.107 --> 00:35:21.030
And if you click on my link,

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.118
you can try it for free yourself.

00:35:23.118 --> 00:35:24.450
And if you like it,

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:28.500
